=== VERDICT ===
  splice (cost model)             3.25 ms
  vs FULL model FP32 ORT CPU      7.51 ms   -> 2.31x
  vs FULL model XINT8 NPU       108.29 ms   -> 33.30x
  backbone alone: NPU 1.71 vs CPU 5.65 ms  -> 3.30x (like-for-like, the honest CNN number)
  attention on CPU is 1.41 ms of the 3.25 ms total (43%), using torch
  with the naive numpy attention instead the same splice is 14.57 ms -> 0.52x, i.e. it LOSES to plain CPU.
  The CPU-side kernel choice, not the NPU, decides the verdict here.

  Reminder: the FP32-CPU comparison is the apples-to-apples one (same ORT,
  same machine, same run). A PyTorch full-model baseline is a different
  measurement and must not be swapped in for this one.

=== Addendum: where the old "18.37 ms CPU baseline" came from ===
Run separately in resnet_env (timm is not installed in resnet_env17), same
machine, same session date. Desktop 2 / Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G.

  torch 2.4.1+cpu, 8 threads
  PyTorch eager FP32 full model: mean 15.71 ms  median 15.24  min 13.94

The previously published splice speedup ("4.1x vs full CPU 18.37 ms") compared an
ORT-executed splice against a PyTorch-eager full-model baseline. Those are two
different runtimes. Measured on the same runtime as the splice, the full FP32
model under the ORT CPU EP is 7.51 ms (see above), so the honest like-for-like
speedup is 2.31x, not 4.1x. The 15.71 ms here is consistent with the old 18.37 ms
figure once run-to-run drift is allowed for -- the baseline was not wrong as a
PyTorch number, it was wrong as the thing to compare against.
